[38;5;231m⏺[39m Using hyperpowers:brainstorming. This is a [1mbounded[0m change — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already exists in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and there's exactly one
  caller — so I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a spec.

  What I found:

  - [38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m — [38;5;153mfunction login(username, password)[39m, a stub that logs and returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m
  - [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m — the only caller, inside the form submit handler
  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — the form has only [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m inputs

  That surfaces the one thing I can't answer from the code: [1mthere is no userId anywhere for the caller to pass.[0m The form
  doesn't collect one, and nothing in the repo produces one. So before I change the signature, I need to know what
  [38;5;153muserId[39m actually is.

  The fork: whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is something the [3mcaller[0m knows before login, or something the [3mlogin[0m knows after it. That
  decides whether this is a parameter at all.

  - [1mClient-generated tracking id[0m — caller mints an id (e.g. a random/session id, or one read from a stored cookie) and
    passes it in. Buys you correlation of login attempts across a session, including failed ones. Costs a new
    id-generation/storage concern in the caller. This is the only option where "parameter" is the right shape.
  - [1mUsername is the identifier[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already receives [38;5;153musername[39m and already logs it. Buys nothing new; the parameter
    would be redundant with data the function has. Cheap to walk back.
  - [1mServer-assigned user id[0m — the real identity of who logged in is known only once the API responds. That makes it a
    [3mreturn value[0m ([38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m) plus logging inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, not a parameter. Changing this later means
    changing the interface again, so it's the expensive one to get wrong.

  My recommendation is the third: "track who logged in" describes the authenticated identity, and the caller provably
  cannot know that before the call. Adding a parameter no caller can fill would mean threading a placeholder through the
  form handler.

